\lessonlabel{2}
\section{Number Systems and Codes}

\begin{note}
\textbf{Learning outcomes.} By the end of this lesson you should be able to:
\begin{itemize}
  \item write numbers in binary, octal and hexadecimal;
  \item convert between decimal, binary, octal and hexadecimal;
  \item explain the purpose of binary coded decimal (BCD);
  \item perform simple BCD encoding and decoding;
  \item describe Gray code and convert between binary and Gray code.
\end{itemize}
\end{note}

\subsection{Binary system}

The \term{binary} system uses only the two digits 0 and 1. Each position represents a power of two, with the right most digit representing \$2^{0}$, the next \$2^{1}$, and so on. A binary number is read from left to right, adding the values of the positions that contain a 1.

\subsection{Octal system}

The \term{octal} system uses the eight digits 0 through 7. Each position represents a power of eight. The right most digit is \$8^{0}$, the next \$8^{1}$, etc. Octal is often used as a compact way to write binary because three binary digits correspond exactly to one octal digit.

\subsection{Hexadecimal system}

The \term{hexadecimal} system uses sixteen symbols: the digits 0 through 9 and the letters A through F to represent the values ten to fifteen. Each position represents a power of sixteen. Hexadecimal is convenient for representing binary data because four binary digits correspond exactly to one hexadecimal digit.

\subsection{Conversions between bases}

\subsubsection{Decimal to binary}

To convert a decimal number to binary, repeatedly divide the number by two, recording the remainder each time. The binary representation is the sequence of remainders read from the last division to the first.

\begin{example}
Convert \$19_{10}$ to binary.
\[
19/2=9\ \text{r}1,\; 9/2=4\ \text{r}1,\; 4/2=2\ \text{r}0,\; 2/2=1\ \text{r}0,\; 1/2=0\ \text{r}1.
\]
Reading the remainders upward gives \$10011_{2}$.
\end{example}

\subsubsection{Binary to hexadecimal}

Group binary digits in fours starting from the right. Replace each group by its hexadecimal equivalent.

\subsubsection{Octal or hexadecimal to binary}

Replace each octal digit by its three bit binary equivalent, or each hexadecimal digit by its four bit binary equivalent.

\subsection{Binary coded decimal (BCD)}

\term{Binary coded decimal} stores each decimal digit separately as a four bit binary number. For example, the decimal number 47 is stored as \$0100\ 0111$ (4 = 0100, 7 = 0111). BCD is useful in devices that need to display decimal digits directly, such as digital clocks and calculators.

\begin{example}
Encode the decimal number 259 in BCD.
\[
2\rightarrow0010,\;5\rightarrow0101,\;9\rightarrow1001,\; \text{so }259_{10}=0010\ 0101\ 1001_{BCD}.
\]
\end{example}

\subsection{Gray code}

\term{Gray code} is a binary numeral system where two successive values differ in only one bit. This property reduces errors in digital communication and position encoding. To obtain the Gray code of a binary number, keep the most significant bit unchanged and XOR each subsequent bit with the bit to its left.

\begin{example}
Convert the binary number \$1011_{2}$ to Gray code.
\[
\text{Most significant bit: }1.\\
\text{Next bit: }1\oplus0=1.\\
\text{Next bit: }0\oplus1=1.\\
\text{Last bit: }1\oplus0=1.
\]
Thus the Gray code is \$1111$.
\end{example}

\begin{keytest}
\textbf{Key test.} Convert \$101101_{2}$ to octal and hexadecimal, then encode the decimal result in BCD.
\end{keytest}

\subsection{Key terms}

\begin{tabular}{@{}p{3.6cm}p{10.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Term} & \textbf{Meaning} \\
\midrule
Binary & Base 2 number system using digits 0 and 1. \\
Octal & Base 8 number system using digits 0--7. \\
Hexadecimal & Base 16 number system using digits 0--9 and A--F. \\
Binary coded decimal (BCD) & Representation of each decimal digit by a four bit binary code. \\
Gray code & Binary code where successive values differ in only one bit. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
